#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dde26e5-909a-5520-b5a4-d567f4f30d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436556} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6e21a7-d420-5add-b68c-d19db196b561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436559} {:answer/id #uuid "ab23a96c-3799-57cf-a6c2-1ae6ae0bc1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436568} {:answer/id #uuid "c12523e2-dee4-5166-961f-0a9b274f76d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436573} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a4e0d8-9189-5dce-8c93-80f07feaa44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436556} :answer-field/id #uuid "1990b86b-b897-5e01-b1d1-8c99349fdebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26e723d1-2e7d-5b20-b4fd-e3325c571238" :question/math-academy-id "q-159696" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3xy-1}{2x-3y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{3xy-1}{2x-3y}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-1)\\cdot (2x-3y)-(3xy-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-3y)}{(2x-3y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x\\cdot (2x-3y)-(3xy-1)\\cdot (-3)}{(2x-3y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-9xy+9xy-3}{(2x-3y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-3}{(2x-3y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(0,-1)} & =\\frac{6(0)^{2}-3}{(2(0)-3(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eb1fcd4-1769-591e-b2b3-2926ae221a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436539} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5aa69d-a57a-53e7-a411-7b8d1e231bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436542} {:answer/id #uuid "8397e509-d007-5ca5-ab7f-b6777f6c8813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436551} {:answer/id #uuid "898b4cbe-f73a-573e-8210-8119141fd3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436553} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f7930b-c4f8-5a1e-9abf-4982ea59ffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436551} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6e9e9d-4e43-54d1-b414-a2a8e2b2fbf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27e4d0e6-6215-57d4-b123-69cd51f3932f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159720" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{xy}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y){e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}y}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{xy}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})\\cdot xy-{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)}{(xy)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\cdot xy-{e}^{xy}\\cdot y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot xy-y{e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}(xy-1)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(xy-1){e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=xy-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2979f6ee-54a4-596c-a00e-b93729a2aa3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436536} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca6a6cc-0e50-532f-b694-62c94e10b39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436537} {:answer/id #uuid "8deb0301-83cb-5ae4-a55a-80df750a6116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436557} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a2557a-94f8-59b3-b60d-cf6d567bf8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436565} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d30be-a6d6-5be3-8d4c-d3456b4912f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436536} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ff5ffb8-ede5-57b1-913a-ec470f862cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "45d0ef52-763b-56d4-b138-648452f050d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132258" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(4,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-x{z}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-{z}^{4}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (-{z}^{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{z}^{4}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(4,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}{|}_{(4,1,1)} & =-(1)^{4}{e}^{4(1)-4(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07492d45-331a-54bb-8e47-f1b2b223890f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436532} {:answer/id #uuid "19558a15-5391-55ca-be3a-0060eedc7548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436534} {:answer/id #uuid "222c0ad5-80fe-5476-9e00-ff1bd21126b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436535} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb64e1-7f9f-5311-8f6d-68fccffe0505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436583} {:answer/id #uuid "e948a45c-9ad5-5281-8231-163c228a69e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436583} :answer-field/id #uuid "23ccffc7-1099-5214-9516-b32cd1948e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c44dfc3-39f6-598a-be48-62e051abdda4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70029" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=(2y+1)^{2x+1},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((2y+1)^{2x+1}) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}((2y+1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\cdot (2\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\cdot (2\\cdot 1+0) \\\\  & =2(2x+1)(2y+1)^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,0)} & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2+1)\\cdot (2\\cdot 0+1)^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5\\cdot {1}^{4} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124ea6fa-c270-53e3-9a4a-37fa95f78b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(2x+{x}^{3}){e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436533} {:answer/id #uuid "7056dc0c-db4a-59ad-96ec-55d08cc43675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}{x}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436549} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd16539-0ba2-553c-a354-7862eff2dcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}(x+{x}^{3}){e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436555} {:answer/id #uuid "9b415193-f30e-568c-b97e-366ae0eaaea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}(x+2{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436562} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfa64ff-4036-5039-b10a-3ab9558904ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436563}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436555} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae5189e-28a0-5ea3-8961-0076527201e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1fee486-b1a7-59bd-ae9a-400ceeb58414" :question/math-academy-id "q-159715" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule and the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}) & ={y}^{2}(\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}})) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}((2x)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (2x)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2{x}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}(x+{x}^{3}){e}^{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da9aa45-1bc3-5a50-8a63-b565f85bcbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436538} {:answer/id #uuid "52dadf7c-60d4-59bf-a211-32c2a6e0ff88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436544} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9bf8eb-7a93-55c9-a108-2de82f52459e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436548} {:answer/id #uuid "a42fffab-44a5-5b6b-b508-53d3e76d2912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436566} {:answer/id #uuid "be5d2acc-cc2d-528d-9424-532714fcefa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436538} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0051c2-f01a-525a-9309-c47735be89a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a235487c-a19a-5a88-ae3b-cb08ed11bcd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-132251" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "738f363f-7faa-5ab8-9897-b5ae98173bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436550} {:answer/id #uuid "a92787e6-66cd-5569-b00d-1c269f0cc548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436567} {:answer/id #uuid "e35b52fb-dc96-5d35-81e4-c054d2cd8c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436581} {:answer/id #uuid "f3882228-8779-5c90-80d2-5aebfcddf4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436587} {:answer/id #uuid "f700fd99-e71c-5d3f-aa39-098cd2e2c465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436581} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb83488-f3c3-5970-b632-d70d187404f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aaa7facc-73c8-58b5-b03f-ad80dd8597f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159691" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product and the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot (4{y}^{3}))) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{4})+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29d2d7f-53ec-597d-89a7-86301ab042db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436564} {:answer/id #uuid "b7315fa2-8e78-5d6e-ae0d-d1ac80caf4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436570} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e3f11a-2a92-578b-8110-66edfa5a659d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436574} {:answer/id #uuid "e45462b7-70a5-5766-8cbb-438381842509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436582} {:answer/id #uuid "f705c608-1802-5751-bc1d-6461a3354b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436582} :answer-field/id #uuid "750b8f34-0f9d-56c2-985a-b6e255c43f41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b72fd759-0cb1-5717-8778-6af4e6d7337a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159653" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)-xy.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)-xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yx) & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot 1-y\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49517556-0d0e-548e-a3c1-8430dc1612ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436543} {:answer/id #uuid "92355996-2538-5d06-adbb-60fbc547dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436560} {:answer/id #uuid "d25dbf87-9ff4-5222-983d-a08125cb25b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436578} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cd43b3-1131-5c70-bf1e-5c97871cb3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436579} {:answer/id #uuid "f122d57b-621e-5f14-a573-0116c8efb68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436578} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b91fb5a-fb60-5758-b55c-b8f8d909486c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f311e12c-1089-5d71-97f6-83e1fd9c3339" :question/math-academy-id "q-159702" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{y}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y})}{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2y\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}{2y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,1)} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(1)^{2}}{2(1)\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+3}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc88c0d-b906-5169-8020-771089d1d342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+7y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436541} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8f703d-08c4-5364-a106-a8206935b823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-11y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436547} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c81c3-bd1b-576f-8ea5-5e15ecc6a989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436558} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa74480-20e7-5b9c-bfdf-57f701ad6150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11y-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436561} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bd98b7-39d1-5926-929c-4305a2b89c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+11y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436561} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba8cfa2f-c627-57d0-923a-8380a85a57e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f88992c6-7672-5688-a790-113b251f1d96" :question/math-academy-id "q-159721" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6x+11y}{\\sqrt{y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{6x+11y}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6x+11y)\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-(6x+11y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y})}{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(11\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-(6x+11y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{22y-(6x+11y)}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{11y-6x}{2y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=11y-6x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c938da8-22ab-5a7b-8a0c-e429c22edff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436540} {:answer/id #uuid "618a305e-63e4-5e63-9489-f0e69a8d45f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436546} {:answer/id #uuid "884b828b-42b8-5163-ae82-8d67dc83a64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436552} {:answer/id #uuid "adda18f8-69d7-5871-aba2-4a24c35225f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436569} {:answer/id #uuid "b77fca6c-dc77-556b-8294-ff448ec939d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436540} :answer-field/id #uuid "e47d5957-7c5b-50d5-a6fa-3e6133a8dcdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fe49ff44-a8f4-5323-af29-4e99b3a5291b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159663" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(x{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,3)} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{1+{1}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1+81} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{82}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7ebbbc-7508-52dc-91d2-56b705b7eccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-12,0,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436986} {:answer/id #uuid "763a0475-ce2e-53a8-aa2d-9466219586f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨12,8,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436989} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e8470-5c37-54ae-b712-f0a64e63b2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,8,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436995} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ae3e99-1ea5-5fa4-a1e9-7139e200dfcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,-4,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436996} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d9044c-103d-5c75-a1cc-b44786993ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-4,0,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436995} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe5a3950-f690-5d04-879c-231b6f838613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3edc7251-4f74-5afa-bcee-22e839f081a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127946" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at $(1,-2,2)$ if $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2z-2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}z+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,2z-2xy,\\,2{x}^{2}z+2y⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-2,2) & =⟨2(1)(2)^{2}-(-2)^{2},\\,-2(1)(-2)+2(2),\\,2(1)^{2}(2)+2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-4,4+4,4-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,8,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a96f20-3d44-594c-b78c-5063ef876b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436981} {:answer/id #uuid "586b4ce1-ff41-581f-8671-763767e3f3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436985} {:answer/id #uuid "e58c9d0d-900d-5547-a140-989e42e2459e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437002} {:answer/id #uuid "f50ec89d-eacd-5a88-8f97-5ca1603aa25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e\\,i+e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437006} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc7838c-e6a9-592d-8e12-48498680e92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436981} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d09cbe-76cd-5f94-9148-15ee4e3c70f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51a75799-7672-5c71-a422-88f2c2c1de7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127652" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(e,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\text{ }i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{ }j, \\\\ ∇f(e,1) & =1(e)^{1-1}\\,i+{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\,j \\\\  & =i+e\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf56146-05fe-53cc-92f2-a535ba4943df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+x(x+y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436998} {:answer/id #uuid "d2acb1a7-39df-54ef-82c2-34bde846d73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+(x+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436999} {:answer/id #uuid "df8dc6a7-9339-5e43-b61c-4513120ec263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437001} {:answer/id #uuid "f072c200-9d5e-5ac5-b478-4233e499425f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437005} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e6b982-9c47-56e1-8b69-4a1e0179c1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+(x+1)(1+y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437005} :answer-field/id #uuid "90c23b7b-1640-5aaa-b8b3-64340afb293e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5961ee6e-358e-5baa-837e-dbd941cc54b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71515" :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $h(x,y)=y{e}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y})={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =x{e}^{y}(y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j)+y{e}^{x}({e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04308c81-1719-5c33-8d40-ebccd5767820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436980} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a1c99-2d0e-5085-aa56-1bc7935a41a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436990} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c28ff-2fd2-5cb7-a89b-d7658bb7354e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436994} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f83b16-7c14-555d-b3bd-510c637f070d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437000} {:answer/id #uuid "e717d850-f7b3-516d-8057-a5abe63141f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436994} :answer-field/id #uuid "798ed032-e7e1-5b90-a986-cd5c9792165f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bace43f6-9821-5763-9bf8-9ca1cf7da037" :question/math-academy-id "q-71353" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,-1)$ if $h(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy},\\,x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-1) & =⟨(2(1)+(-1)){e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)},\\,1\\cdot {e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e36d0b8-dad3-53f0-905d-1b73b0cfb007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436982} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb3ce4f-3615-5638-9cca-7d5f9a7f4065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436983} {:answer/id #uuid "4a50ec3f-8ef4-53d6-b614-888ffb598364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436984} {:answer/id #uuid "b421e42f-f55a-5ffe-a533-367af3998505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1)$ and $(-1,-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436993} {:answer/id #uuid "ca48ce3d-8118-5a85-8d40-9f54dccebe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-1)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436983} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f85d81-d1f0-5077-ba52-dc9b36b72fa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c3503a39-2405-5c8f-9ad7-2d39b5c187d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-128513" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =2x-2{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4z-4yx \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4y-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-{y}^{2} &=0, \\\\ z-yx &=0, \\\\ y-z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe first equation gives $x={y}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain $z={y}^{3}.$ Substituting $z={y}^{3}$ into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=(-1)^{2}=1$ and $z=(-1)^{3}=-1.$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we obtain $x={0}^{2}=0$ and $z={0}^{3}=0.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ gives $x={1}^{2}=1$ and $z={1}^{3}=1.$\n\nFinally, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at three points: $(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5c93cec-cb82-55b3-b6d7-62d69fb76aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439901} {:answer/id #uuid "2c99ad9c-faf3-5acd-bdb2-ee27ef1f4fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4119791-ba8c-5e0c-a64d-e772d7d2a0ad" :answer-field/key "$(-1,0,-2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0add57ab-bb1a-55eb-b596-3b77efd157fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439903} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fce83d-df09-578a-a9d6-c591f2f04aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439904}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439904} :answer-field/id #uuid "48fff79f-e183-546b-875a-d5076fd6b4db" :answer-field/key "$(0,0,-2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0a7a9d-80cf-5743-9a4e-10f145820cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439905} {:answer/id #uuid "d56042bf-0a2b-5929-8c2d-29272635d397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439906} :answer-field/id #uuid "92428694-af1e-5eb5-bc74-f8cb0aa0c761" :answer-field/key "$(0,0,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0059f0f-4a6f-5caa-9dba-9936a289c5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439907} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3870f9-c0c6-5caa-b070-c262ba11e1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439907} :answer-field/id #uuid "518a9fff-84eb-58e5-b9d5-a58af9766148" :answer-field/key "$(0,0,2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d13fce5d-8bd4-547a-afec-720ff5befaa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71470" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y(x+1){e}^{x}=0 \\\\ x{e}^{x}=0 \\\\ z(2+z){e}^{z}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe second equation gives $x=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0+1){e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get  $z=0$ or $z=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee2cdb4-9a34-5a8b-a223-2ea4a089aa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437208} {:answer/id #uuid "4c763f52-9b23-5041-b90c-5411e27f7574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4uv(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437209} {:answer/id #uuid "962f026a-a6e1-5e0c-8e81-b3f195d58e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437210} {:answer/id #uuid "97d54cc4-bb7e-5809-af16-75ed6fdc3a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437211} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2ceb4d-c670-5d4a-ba41-be274f566d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{u}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437208} :answer-field/id #uuid "07041bd7-da85-5245-8203-4f70786b0ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdaff75b-e0c6-5e92-b720-ea0f3e9d5336" :question/math-academy-id "q-81263" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v \\\\ {u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v$ and $y={u}^{2}{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{u}{v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2{u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v & \\frac{u}{v} \\\\ 2u{v}^{2} & 2{u}^{2}v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot 2{u}^{2}v-\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot 2u{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cd3b229-87d5-5fe0-bf43-96e6859b0b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447338} {:answer/id #uuid "8cda681d-6db9-54b3-bd78-3fc12602c207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447340} {:answer/id #uuid "bd84b736-12f3-529d-aed6-fa8d36f93fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447346} {:answer/id #uuid "c943eb76-7894-538d-b2d8-85356d2d0217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447350} {:answer/id #uuid "caf722cf-879e-502b-9dcd-ed9b635a2c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447346} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d61b3aa-4d6d-50f6-8ee7-8efa72e96369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c716d26-2571-5872-95a3-6c53428692e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130102" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y)$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,\\pi ) & =2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{2}-\\pi )\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{2}-\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(0,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x-0,\\,y-\\pi ⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269f786d-89ec-5cd5-a946-ff7a25f5bfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447331} {:answer/id #uuid "64cdcf40-7d0f-5cd1-9631-68535525b11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447337} {:answer/id #uuid "9652c686-4a06-5958-9b68-3ac0c59d9c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447342} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b197ac-be86-5a79-bafe-30616fa5194f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{5+\\sqrt{5}}{2},\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447349} {:answer/id #uuid "f223ad97-8210-5437-a5b5-584c17f1bb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447337} :answer-field/id #uuid "4746e6c2-e2ff-5920-bf56-69cc85ac1d10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2ac861e6-ea7e-53f0-b9c2-bba93cd70561" :question/math-academy-id "q-130227" :question/problem "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=xy$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(-2y-x)\\,i-(2x-y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((-2y-x)\\,i-(2x-y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ -2y-x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2y$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =xy \\\\ (-2y)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(-2y)y \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $y=0,$ we get $x=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9548efa7-b337-5098-83fd-68445ff80070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447341} {:answer/id #uuid "c60ad1d3-95a7-5a9f-b2b9-64da4df122a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447348} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e79c61-6c42-5702-a833-be284f78ce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i+5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447358} {:answer/id #uuid "f692be5a-8425-517e-a612-23b54fc9103d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i-4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447361} {:answer/id #uuid "f8735719-d369-5e0e-a1c4-c3a71df5f0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447348} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d8ed8e-549b-58fd-a410-9d0b085953e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43788191-a65e-5058-bbfd-37ff934a1aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72403" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $(x-y)^{2}=2x-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2} & =2x-y \\\\ (x-y)^{2}-2x+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}-2x+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,0)={f}_{y}(2,0)i-{f}_{x}(2,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)j \\\\  & =(-2(x-y)+1)i-(2(x-y)-2)j \\\\ t(2,0) & =(-2((2)-(0))+1)\\,i-((2((2)-(0))-2)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-3\\,i-2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16af8390-5001-553a-b0f4-0d97c561270f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨11,-10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447330} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9a977b-a2a7-597d-813d-3e1b77c5b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨12,14⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447332} {:answer/id #uuid "bc35064f-3e58-53ec-9341-b4fdb731d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447345} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a87d66-7787-5592-a30b-ee6c5d027bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨6,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447355} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd368a-c465-55d9-8a07-4d1ad01503a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨10,-11⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447357} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5737f4-7f36-5112-b8c8-5f6aec3f9ccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d7bf3ca-d3c4-5a27-836b-a5e78d17fbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-36397" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(3y+4)\\,i+(3x+6-10y)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(3(2)+4)\\,i+(3(1)+6-10(2))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i-11\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,-11⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9a2db1-529d-5628-9061-7052c51aa8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447347} {:answer/id #uuid "cc49cf9f-93a9-5729-904e-6ab3eebbf63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447352} {:answer/id #uuid "cf37dabf-a5f5-5488-94e7-124530583cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447353} {:answer/id #uuid "f66bf744-8b15-5c45-869d-f7226d3e3a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447360} {:answer/id #uuid "fa96e6a2-688c-5137-81fa-3a6eabc7070a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447352} :answer-field/id #uuid "16678482-e75d-56bd-a681-d31f80ad72e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "caca4712-f17f-5ed4-8f57-6c6c5c27d1ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-130213" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $x{y}^{2}=3-2y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{y}^{2}+2y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2xy+2 & =0 \\\\ xy+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =3-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =3+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get $y=3,$ and the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{3},3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041af4e2-416c-520b-860e-976073e922ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458624} {:answer/id #uuid "0aaf9612-44d8-54bc-9d28-79d0bc49b6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458639} {:answer/id #uuid "726ded84-7042-51fb-99ed-92eea059caa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{4},-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458826} {:answer/id #uuid "96c64968-3e1c-5d8a-b152-bcd832a0e258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{2}{3})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458892} {:answer/id #uuid "d27915a1-c3cc-5b5c-85e5-d0ff3c9758a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458624} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42e75fa-5b9c-5ff9-b5fc-949249e16a12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49174b89-3c43-532e-8951-fbe13dc1efa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130221" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy=2x-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $2\\,i-j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy & =2x-y \\\\ 2xy-2x+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-2x+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2x+y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i-(2y-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $2\\,i-j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (2\\,i-j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (2\\,i-j) & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\,i-(2y-2)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i-j) & =0 \\\\ (4x+2)+(2y-2) & =0 \\\\ 4x+2y & =0 \\\\ y & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-2x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy & =2x-y \\\\ 2x(-2x) & =2x-(-2x) \\\\ -4{x}^{2} & =4x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47412d9d-c711-5221-8676-db85828bba36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-5,6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458757} {:answer/id #uuid "8e879653-b53c-5991-8c3c-66b0de773e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458876} {:answer/id #uuid "99eeb82f-f1e9-5ccc-a668-61e185f655d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-12,16⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458899} {:answer/id #uuid "aa41c157-f1af-54f7-be89-0b4db8719009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,18⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458934} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b40389-df84-5d28-9b01-a8cf1618fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458876} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c8d44b-dce1-5e85-acdf-a5ba9f5b5fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b652bc17-f344-5009-a0c7-148f214aa2f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-36394" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+5xy-5x{y}^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+5xy-5x{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+5xy-5x{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy+5y-5{y}^{2})i+({x}^{2}+5x-10xy)j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,2) & =(2(3)(2)+5(2)-5(2)^{2})i+((3)^{2}+5(3)-10(3)(2))j \\\\  & =2\\,i-36\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-36⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,2)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1771b53b-a9ea-5204-9e6c-c3664e7f4eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475475} {:answer/id #uuid "52b82999-7d0c-5f60-b09d-5d9bfb9dabdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475563} {:answer/id #uuid "644db317-5ce2-5b55-a3a9-b57771242cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475590} {:answer/id #uuid "9e112b0b-f642-5793-ba13-d921da5ca298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475677} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d267a1-bd0b-57f4-92ed-545ceb83bbea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475475} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe5df83-a1f1-5212-94d6-8a03a2936996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83c8f2ad-db17-53f8-868d-e48deabc8feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129409" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=2$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=x+1$\n\nIII. The limit does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{(1-1)(2-2)}{(1-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0(0)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(1,2)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=2}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(2-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(0)}{(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=x+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=x+1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x+1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x-1)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}}{2\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f9824-699a-5a38-83d3-40267dc46ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480190} {:answer/id #uuid "31a44f88-a9d2-5ac1-9074-4eaba651ac12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480191} {:answer/id #uuid "9f74324e-554a-5e36-8177-d8ee9a8eb1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480372} {:answer/id #uuid "ce889001-65f4-5414-8ef9-dfcfe539055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480456} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ca569d-59b2-52ea-96f3-06e9ff60d259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480191} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3aefe0-abfb-587a-90a2-c75247bc2342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f10acf7-191a-5a7b-ad4b-e4a80fb0c241" :question/math-academy-id "q-101521" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=6\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta ={30}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28e79a2a-f934-5d02-bb33-fed7a4c61fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480179} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d844d6a-b86e-5583-9197-f2e6b196cc47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b93ffaa5-a268-5040-8e2a-c610ae7ae03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-275242" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=7$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={90}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fd4f57-3f0a-5ebc-aa07-bb84afb9cc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480833} {:answer/id #uuid "3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480861} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1e541a-a0b5-50e1-8a46-d0f83df1d6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481124} {:answer/id #uuid "e902cbe7-031f-5540-a22a-6e30754fbd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481184} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe6534a-9156-5f3a-a3f4-adabfe81028f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480861} :answer-field/id #uuid "149fcd69-9998-58e5-a043-dbc9ef09dd8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554" :question/math-academy-id "q-160937" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{2}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(1,1+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{2}{3}) & =(2,2+3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(2,2+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a70e77-e143-5fe1-987f-6cf3d9622cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480759} {:answer/id #uuid "16926c3a-001d-572a-beb5-ac09cba7435e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480780} {:answer/id #uuid "930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481014} {:answer/id #uuid "943844b2-6990-576d-9141-b10699c96298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481018} {:answer/id #uuid "96c98676-4b18-52e8-baf0-b525847f1830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481014} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dca1b12-338a-5c5f-badf-6213000ef5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" :question/math-academy-id "q-160903" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,1),$ $(3,0),$ and $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
